@engagement = http://localhost:4002
@identity = http://localhost:4001

### Casino A deposit webhook (workflow steps 1-3)
# Signature: x-a-signature = hex HMAC-SHA256(hmac_secret, "${ts}.${rawBody}"), x-a-timestamp = ts (epoch seconds).
# The timestamp must be within +-300 s of the server clock, so sign a fresh one; the fixed value below only
# shows the shape and answers 401 "stale" when sent as is. The casino simulator signs this for you.
# The tenant is resolved from the slug in the path only. The body takes exactly these fields (no others).
POST {{engagement}}/v1/integrations/casino-a-demo/events
Content-Type: application/json
x-a-timestamp: 1790000000
x-a-signature: {{casino_a_signature}}

{"eventId":"evt-a-0001","playerId":"A-PLAYER-77","amount":{"value":5000,"currency":"EUR"},"status":"SUCCESS","ts":1790000000}

# Response (shape from the contract, not captured):
# HTTP/1.1 200 OK
# { "status": "accepted", "inbound_event_id": "0190...-inbound" }
#
# What happens next (asynchronously, over the event bus):
#   engagement.deposit.confirmed -> rules engine -> spin credit (available) -> engagement.spin.granted
# The deposit rule in the demo tenant: deposit >= 2000 minor units grants 1 spin.

### A non-successful status is acknowledged and ignored (no row, no event)
POST {{engagement}}/v1/integrations/casino-a-demo/events
Content-Type: application/json
x-a-timestamp: 1790000001
x-a-signature: {{casino_a_signature_pending}}

{"eventId":"evt-a-0002","playerId":"A-PLAYER-77","amount":{"value":5000,"currency":"EUR"},"status":"PENDING","ts":1790000001}

# Response (shape from the contract, not captured):
# HTTP/1.1 200 OK
# { "status": "ignored", "inbound_event_id": null }

### Player launch: the casino signs a launch token, MIO exchanges it for a player session (public endpoint)
# The launch token is an HS256 JWT signed with the credential's hmac_secret (see 01), header { "alg": "HS256", "typ": "JWT", "kid": "<key_id>" },
# claims { "pid": "<casino player id>", "plat": "casino-a", "aud": "mio-launch", "iat": <now>, "exp": <now + 120>, "jti": "<unique, 8+ chars>" }.
# It is single use. The session token it returns is valid for identity, engagement and payments, so one
# {{player_token}} serves every player call in these files. (scripts/demo.sh builds the token with openssl.)
# @name launch
POST {{identity}}/v1/auth/launch
Content-Type: application/json

{ "token": "{{casino_launch_token}}" }

# Response (shape from the contract, not captured):
# HTTP/1.1 200 OK
# { "access_token": "<jwt>", "token_type": "Bearer", "expires_in": 900, "user_id": "0190...-player", "tenant_id": "0190...-tenant-a" }

### How many spins does the player hold? (player token from the launch exchange)
GET {{engagement}}/v1/me/spins
Authorization: Bearer {{player_token}}

# Response (captured from scripts/demo.sh, after a deposit of 5000 minor units):
# HTTP/1.1 200 OK
# {"available":1}
